        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. lambda And Callables"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`lambda x: x*2` is the same `types.FunctionType` as a `def`",
            "Difference is syntax: one expression, no statement body",
            "Callable is wider than function: anything with `__call__`",
            "Test with `callable(obj)`, not an isinstance check"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "class Twice:",
            "    def __call__(self, x): return x*2",
            "callable(Twice())   # True, not a function"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Nested Defs Are Fresh"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "An inner `def` runs on every call of the outer function",
            "New function object, new captured cells, each time",
            "Name lookup order: Local, Enclosing, Global, Built-in",
            "Enclosing means an outer *function*, never the module"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Why Decorators Can Exist",
          "text": "Pass a function in, return a replacement out. The whole chapter rests on functions being ordinary objects."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Assignment Decides Scope",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Assign anywhere in the body: the name is local everywhere in it",
            "Decided at compile time from syntax, not from runtime values",
            "Reading a global is fine — until you also assign to it",
            "The read on line 1 fails because of line 3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "x = 1",
            "def f():",
            "    print(x)   # UnboundLocalError",
            "    x = 2      # this line made x local"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. global vs nonlocal"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Keyword",
            "Rebinds",
            "Fails when"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`global x`",
              "module name",
              "never — it creates it"
            ],
            [
              "`nonlocal x`",
              "nearest outer function",
              "no such binding exists"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Functions Are Objects (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def outer():",
            "    n = 0",
            "    def inner():",
            "        nonlocal n; n += 1; return n",
            "    return inner   # counter, no class"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Missing outer name: `SyntaxError: no binding for nonlocal 'x' found`",
            "`global` in a nested def still jumps straight to module level",
            "Neither keyword is needed merely to mutate"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Mutate vs Rebind"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Statement",
            "Needs nonlocal?"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`lst.append(1)`",
              "no — mutation"
            ],
            [
              "`d['k'] = 1`",
              "no — mutation"
            ],
            [
              "`lst = [1]`",
              "yes — rebinding"
            ],
            [
              "`n += 1`",
              "yes — rebinding"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Closure, Stated Plainly"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Inner function plus the captured bindings of its enclosing scope",
            "Those bindings outlive the outer call that made them",
            "Three conditions: nested def, reads an outer name, gets returned"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def make_adder(n):",
            "    def add(x): return x + n   # n is free",
            "    return add",
            "add5 = make_adder(5); add5(3)   # 8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Open The Cells"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "add5.__closure__          # (<cell: int 5>,)",
            "add5.__closure__[0].cell_contents  # 5",
            "add5.__code__.co_freevars   # ('n',)",
            "make_adder.__code__.co_cellvars  # ('n',)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Free vs Cell",
          "text": "`co_freevars` lists what the inner borrows. `co_cellvars` lists what the outer exports."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Cells Hold Names, Not Values",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Capture is by reference — the cell is shared, never copied",
            "The read happens at call time, not at definition time",
            "So a later rebinding is visible inside the closure"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "fs = [lambda: i for i in range(3)]",
            "[f() for f in fs]   # [2, 2, 2]  — one cell",
            "fs = [lambda i=i: i for i in range(3)]",
            "[f() for f in fs]   # [0, 1, 2]  — bound now"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Functions Are Objects (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. One Call, One Set Of Cells"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A factory call is the other fix: `make_adder(i)` per item",
            "`make_adder(5)` and `make_adder(9)` share nothing at all",
            "Closures pin their captures alive — memory stays held"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Closure Or Class?"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Need",
            "Reach for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "one behaviour",
              "closure"
            ],
            [
              "named state",
              "class"
            ],
            [
              "several methods",
              "class"
            ],
            [
              "terse, no class needed",
              "closure"
            ],
            [
              "named state, inspection, picklable",
              "class"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. @ Is Literally Rebinding",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "@deco",
            "def f(): ...",
            "# byte-for-byte identical to:",
            "def f(): ...",
            "f = deco(f)      # runs right after the def"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A decorator is any callable taking one function, returning a replacement",
            "Decorator body runs once at import; wrapper body runs per call",
            "The module name now points at the wrapper",
            "The original survives only as the closed-over `fn`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Minimal Wrapper"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def deco(fn):",
            "    def wrapper(*a, **kw):",
            "        return fn(*a, **kw)   # keep this!",
            "    return wrapper"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Silent None",
          "text": "Drop that inner `return` and every decorated call quietly yields None. No error, no traceback, no clue."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Any Callable Expression"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Since PEP 614: `@registry[name]`, `@obj.method`, `@make_deco(x)`",
            "Works on module functions, nested defs, methods and classes",
            "Nothing magic about `@` — it is placement, not power"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Scope, nonlocal & Cells",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Wrapper-Free Decorators",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "REG = {}",
            "def register(fn):",
            "    REG[fn.__name__] = fn",
            "    return fn      # unchanged, no wrapper"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Side-effect decorators just record and return `fn`",
            "No wrapper means no metadata loss and no extra frame",
            "Patch after import too: `mod.f = deco(mod.f)`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Naive Wrappers Erase Identity",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Attribute",
            "Bare wrapper",
            "With wraps"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`__name__`",
              "'wrapper'",
              "'greet'"
            ],
            [
              "`__doc__`",
              "None",
              "original"
            ],
            [
              "`__qualname__`",
              "deco.<locals>.wrapper",
              "greet"
            ],
            [
              "`__wrapped__`",
              "absent",
              "the function"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. The Fix Is One Line"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "from functools import wraps",
            "def deco(fn):",
            "    @wraps(fn)",
            "    def wrapper(*a, **kw):",
            "        return fn(*a, **kw)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Copies `__module__`, `__name__`, `__qualname__`, `__doc__`",
            "`__dict__` is updated, not replaced",
            "Sets `__wrapped__ = fn` for later traversal",
            "Thin shell over `update_wrapper(wrapper, fn)`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Tunable And Traversable"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`WRAPPER_ASSIGNMENTS` / `WRAPPER_UPDATES` are editable tuples",
            "`inspect.signature(f)` follows `__wrapped__` to the real signature",
            "`follow_wrapped=False` shows the wrapper's own `(*a, **kw)`",
            "`inspect.unwrap(f)` walks back to the undecorated function"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. What wraps Cannot Do"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Copies references only — `__code__` still binds `*a, **kw`",
            "The signature is reported, never enforced",
            "On builtins nothing raises — ==missing attrs are skipped== since 3.2",
            "`@wraps(len)` works; the copied metadata is just **thin**",
            "`AttributeError` only if the *wrapper* rejects attribute assignment"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Who Reads This Metadata",
          "text": "help(), pydoc, Sphinx autodoc, pytest test ids, debugger tracebacks. All of them say 'wrapper' without wraps."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Then Forward Everything"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):",
            "    return fn(*args, **kwargs)  # relay all"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Capture Traps & @ Sugar",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Standing Rule",
          "text": "Every decorator that returns a new function gets @wraps. No exceptions, no judgement call."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Collect, Then Unpack",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Site",
            "`*a` does",
            "`**kw` does"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "def line",
              "tuple of positionals",
              "dict of keywords"
            ],
            [
              "call line",
              "unpacks the tuple",
              "unpacks the dict"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def w(*args, **kwargs):",
            "    print(args, kwargs)  # ((1,), {'b': 2})",
            "    return fn(*args, **kwargs)",
            "w(1, b=2)   # same syntax, opposite way"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Reading One Parameter Safely"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "b = inspect.signature(fn).bind(*a, **kw)",
            "b.apply_defaults()",
            "user = b.arguments['user']  # call-style proof"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Never index `args[0]` blindly — callers may use a keyword",
            "For methods `args[0]` is `self`, not your first parameter",
            "`bind` normalises both call styles into one mapping"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Editing In Flight"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "args = (args[0].strip(),) + args[1:]  # rebuild",
            "result = fn(*args, **kwargs)",
            "return result.upper()   # transform after"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Match The Callable Kind"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Wrapped thing",
            "Wrapper must"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "sync def",
              "`return fn(*a, **kw)`"
            ],
            [
              "async def",
              "`await fn(*a, **kw)`"
            ],
            [
              "generator",
              "`yield from fn(*a, **kw)`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`functools.partial` pre-binds args when you need no behaviour change",
            "A narrow `def wrapper(x)` breaks every caller passing extras"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Decorators That Take Args",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "@repeat(3)",
            "def f(): ...",
            "# means: f = repeat(3)(f)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`repeat(3)` is called first and must return a decorator",
            "Three levels: factory(args), decorator(fn), wrapper(*a, **kw)",
            "Factory arguments ride the closure into every wrapper call"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Factory Skeleton, Top"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def repeat(n):          # level 1: factory",
            "    def deco(fn):       # level 2: decorator",
            "        @wraps(fn)",
            "        def w(*a, **kw):  # level 3: wrapper"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Keeping Identity: functools.wraps",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Forgetting The Call",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`@repeat` binds your function to `n` — no error at that line",
            "It explodes later inside `range(n)`, far from the cause",
            "Validate at factory time so the raise lands on the decorator"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def repeat(n):",
            "    if not isinstance(n, int):",
            "        raise TypeError('repeat(n) needs an int')"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Bare Or Called, Both"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def deco(fn=None, *, x=1):",
            "    if fn is None:",
            "        return partial(deco, x=x)  # @deco(x=2)",
            "    ...                            # plain @deco"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Factory Shapes You Already Use"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "Configures"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`@lru_cache(maxsize=128)`",
              "cache size"
            ],
            [
              "`@app.route('/path')`",
              "URL rule"
            ],
            [
              "`@retry(times=3, delay=1)`",
              "attempts"
            ],
            [
              "`@pytest.mark.parametrize(...)`",
              "test matrix"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. A Class As Decorator",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "class Counted:",
            "    def __init__(self, fn):",
            "        self.fn = fn; self.count = 0",
            "        update_wrapper(self, fn)  # not @wraps"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Keyword-only options make the two shapes unambiguous",
            "The factory runs once per decorated function",
            "Never park mutable defaults in factory scope — all share them"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Instances are callable, so the class is a valid decorator",
            "`self.count` beats juggling `nonlocal` counters",
            "`@wraps` decorates functions; instances need `update_wrapper`"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Keeping Identity: functools.wraps (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. The Method Problem"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A class-decorated method loses binding — no `self` arrives",
            "The instance is skipped unless you implement `__get__`",
            "Or sidestep it: use a function-based decorator on methods"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def __get__(self, obj, objtype=None):",
            "    return functools.partial(self.__call__, obj)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Decorating A Class Itself"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`@deco class C` means `C = deco(C)`",
            "The decorator may mutate, subclass or replace the class",
            "Stdlib: `@dataclass`, `@total_ordering`, `@runtime_checkable`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Built-In Four",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Decorator",
            "First arg",
            "Access"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`@staticmethod`",
              "none",
              "`C.f()`"
            ],
            [
              "`@classmethod`",
              "`cls`",
              "`C.f()`"
            ],
            [
              "`@property`",
              "`self`",
              "`obj.f`"
            ],
            [
              "`@cached_property`",
              "`self`",
              "computed once"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`cached_property` stores the value in the instance `__dict__`",
            "So a `__slots__` class without `__dict__` cannot use it",
            "`@classmethod` returns a descriptor, so it must be outermost"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Applied Bottom-Up"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "@a",
            "@b",
            "def f(): ...",
            "# f = a(b(f))   # b wraps first"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Executed Top-Down"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "call f()",
              "a wrapper",
              "b wrapper",
              "real f"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Closest to `def` wraps first — it is innermost",
            "Highest in the file runs first — it is outermost",
            "All application side effects fire at import, bottom-up"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. When Order Bites",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Stack",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`@cache` over `@log`",
              "repeats never log"
            ],
            [
              "`@log` over `@cache`",
              "logs hits too"
            ],
            [
              "`@timer` over `@retry`",
              "total elapsed"
            ],
            [
              "`@retry` over `@timer`",
              "per attempt"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`@app.route` outermost — registration must see the final function",
